Your evening class has a small Unix server. The instructor gives you an
account so you can work on the same files from home and the classroom.
The first connection asks whether you trust a key you have never seen.
The sensible response is not to guess what the computer wants you to say.
It is to know what would count as an answer. The printed server names
in this chapter are examples: all hands-on work here can be done offline.

\begin{objectives}
\item Read an SSH destination and identify which account and machine it names.
\item Verify the server's identity before authenticating yourself.
\item Tell a remote shell from your local shell and close it deliberately.
\end{objectives}

\section{Obtain the connection details first}\label{sec:38-details}
\index{ssh@\texttt{ssh}}\index{secure shell}\index{remote shell}
SSH, or Secure Shell, gives you a protected connection to a service on
another machine. What you do after connecting depends on what the account
permits. An ordinary login often starts a shell much like the one at your
desk. The terminal window stays on your machine; commands at the new
prompt run as your remote account. In Chapter~\ref{ch:37}, the URL told a
browser several things about a request. Here you supply the connection
details as command arguments.
Figure~\ref{fig:38-destination} labels the parts of a nondefault-port
connection before you have to type them.

Ask the account provider for the exact server name, your remote account
name, any nondefault port, the expected host key fingerprint and key type,
and the approved sign-in method. Find out whether the provider requires
an institutional network or a VPN. A VPN is a connection into another
network; it can make an otherwise inaccessible service available, but
does not replace checking the service's identity. Only connect to services
you are explicitly allowed to use. Do not point these commands at a
different machine when the example name doesn't work.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering\input{figures/chapter-38/destination.tikz}
\caption{The username belongs to the account on the destination.}\label{fig:38-destination}
\VisualDescription{One label identifies the SSH port, one the account, and one the host. The account label is not the laptop's login.}
\end{figure}

For a standard-port login the shape of the command is:

\begin{terminal}[title={Command form only: do not run with the example name}]
ssh learner@unix.example.org
\end{terminal}

The \texttt{@} separates the remote user \texttt{learner} from the host
\texttt{unix.example.org}. If you leave the user out, SSH normally uses
your local username unless its configuration says otherwise. That can
be surprising on a laptop named for you but an account named for your
class. A port the provider explicitly gives you goes after the lowercase
\texttt{-p} option; here, \texttt{2222} is an example, not a suggested
alternative to try when the connection fails:

\begin{terminal}[title={Command form only}]
ssh -p 2222 learner@unix.example.org
\end{terminal}

OpenSSH is the SSH implementation used in these examples. It is not a
POSIX utility; check availability and local behavior first on Linux,
macOS, FreeBSD, OpenBSD, or NetBSD. The following reads the client
version without opening a connection. The uppercase \texttt{-V} matters:
lowercase \texttt{-v} is a different option that requests verbose output.
Some terminals show no difference between output streams, but if you
redirect version output and the file is empty, standard error may be
where the line went.

\begin{terminal}
ssh -V
\end{terminal}

You can also inspect what the OpenSSH client would use, offline. In this
specific example \texttt{-F /dev/null} supplies an empty alternative
configuration file and suppresses the usual configuration files,
\texttt{-G} prints the effective configuration and exits,
\texttt{-T} disables terminal allocation for this inspection, and
\texttt{-p 2222} sets the requested port. Use the empty configuration
only for this exercise, not as advice to ignore your provider's settings.
\cmd|grep -E| selects lines by an extended regular expression;
\cmd|^| anchors the match to the start of a line, and the alternatives
name three fields, each followed by one space.

\begin{terminal}
ssh -F /dev/null -G -T -p 2222 learner@unix.example.org |
  grep -E '^(host|user|port) '
\end{terminal}

The result should include three fields: \texttt{host unix.example.org},
\texttt{user learner}, and \texttt{port 2222}. It proves only how the
client read these arguments, not that this host exists or has an account
for you. If the client rejects \texttt{-G}, check \cmd|man ssh| rather
than deleting the option and inadvertently opening a connection.
Avoid casually substituting a bare \texttt{ssh -G} on a real host:
configuration rules can invoke helper commands or perform name lookup.
Also avoid posting the entire generated configuration, which can expose
private paths or institutional settings.

\begin{quicktable}{p{1.35in}Y}
\toprule
Information & Place to get it, or check it \\
\midrule
Host and port & A service owner or official setup instructions; not a guess based on an IP address. \\
Remote account & The provider's enrollment details; may not match your local username. \\
Host fingerprint & A trusted support channel or an authenticated provider page independent of the new connection. \\
Sign-in method & Your account provider's instructions; a password, a user key, or an additional verification step may be required. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\section{The server identifies itself before you sign in}
\index{host key}\index{fingerprint}\index{known hosts}
On the first connection OpenSSH might report something like this
illustrative excerpt. The ellipsis represents omitted characters;
real checks compare the entire fingerprint and the indicated key type.
The default prompt can vary with the client and its configuration.

\begin{terminaloutput}
The authenticity of host '[unix.example.org]:2222' can't be established.
ED25519 key fingerprint is SHA256:...
Are you sure you want to continue connecting (yes/no/[fingerprint])?
\end{terminaloutput}

A \FirstUse{host key} is a key through which the server can demonstrate
its identity. The \FirstUse{fingerprint} is a compact identifier made
from the public key so you don't have to compare the entire key. A
fingerprint displayed by the connection is a claim, not independent
proof about whose key it is. Get the expected fingerprint from the
provider before trusting the connection, and compare the fingerprint and
type it actually reports. A server can have different host keys for
different algorithms; if the owner supplied only a fingerprint for the
wrong key type, ask for the corresponding trusted fingerprint.
Figure~\ref{fig:38-identity} separates that evidence from the key
claim shown by the connection.

After you confirm the fingerprint and accept the key, SSH can record that
identity in a known-hosts file, normally \texttt{\string~/.ssh/known\_hosts}
for OpenSSH's user file; system-wide records and configuration can also
apply. In future the client can compare what it sees with what it already
knows. A connection without a first-use prompt may already be covered by
a trusted host key record or the provider's managed configuration. It
is not a reason to change that configuration.

What counts as an independent channel? The course's access instructions
inside its already authenticated learning system may be enough. So
might calling the computing desk on a number you obtained when you
registered, rather than one provided by a surprise prompt or mail.
Two copies of a fingerprint copied from the same new connection aren't
two channels. If all you have is the unfamiliar display, answer
\texttt{no} or cancel and ask. Postponing a first login is less work
than investigating whether you gave a password to a stranger.

Preserve the distinction between a service name and a host key
when moving between devices. A login that you verified yesterday on
your workstation doesn't automatically create a trusted record
on your new laptop. Repeat the approved verification or use the
provider-managed host records. On shared systems avoid accepting a
prompt on behalf of someone else just because you're at the keyboard.
This is also why the key in a legitimate service-change announcement
should be checked independently. An urgent-looking announcement by
itself is not stronger evidence than an urgent-looking prompt.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering\input{figures/chapter-38/identity.tikz}
\caption{Check host identity independently of account authentication.}\label{fig:38-identity}
\VisualDescription{Compare the server's key claim with a fingerprint obtained independently. Only after a match does the example proceed to account sign-in.}
\end{figure}

\begin{warningbox}[If a known host changes]
A rebuilt server can legitimately have a new key. An impostor can also
present a different key. Stop on an unexpected change and report it
through the provider's known channel. Do not disable host checks, delete
all known-hosts entries, or remove the old entry just to silence the
warning. After the provider confirms a rotation, follow their instructions
to update the specific verified record.
\end{warningbox}

Encryption and identity are separate ideas. Encrypting a conversation
protects it along the route, but you still need to know who is at the
other end. Typing a password after accepting a stranger's first key is
like telling the wrong person your password in an otherwise private
room. Passwords or codes belong only at the approved sign-in prompt
after the server identity check. Never put them in a command operand,
shell variable demo, or configuration comment. A password prompt may
display no characters while you type; that can be normal.

\begin{dialoguebox}{Two keys, two questions}
\Brian{I thought the key proves who I am. Why does the server have one?}
\Stephen{SSH checks a key to establish whom it is talking to.}
\Brian{You said ``it.'' Which end gets to check whom?}
\Stephen{You're right: there are two checks. Your client checks the server's host key. The server then checks your account credentials.}
\Brian{So knowing the server's fingerprint doesn't get me into my account.}
\Stephen{No more than recognizing your bank gives you a bank account.}
\Brian{And my personal key can't prove I've reached the right server.}
\Stephen{Exactly; a strong sign-in method doesn't replace the first check.}
\DialogueConclusion{Check the host, then prove your own identity.}
\end{dialoguebox}

\section{Learn what a public key file looks like}
\index{ssh-keygen@\texttt{ssh-keygen}}\index{public key}\index{private key}
A user key is a pair. A service can authorize the public part for your
account; the client uses the private part to demonstrate possession
without sending that private key to the server. In an ordinary OpenSSH
key pair on disk the public file ends in \texttt{.pub}; the matching
filename without that suffix is normally the private file. The
passphrase protecting a local private key, when used, is not your account
password, and is not sent to the server. For a real enrollment, follow
the provider's key type, protection, and upload instructions. Use a
passphrase for a real user key unless your provider requires another
approved method of protection. Do not copy a private key to a server or
submit it in a support ticket. A comment on the public key can identify
you, so even public keys need not be posted indiscriminately.

For optional practice, use a shell that accepts POSIX \texttt{sh}
syntax and make a fresh local directory. Here \texttt{-d} makes
a directory and \texttt{mktemp} replaces the final \texttt{X}s
with a unique suffix. The example uses the system's local
\texttt{/tmp}; use a different trusted absolute temporary-directory
path if your system requires one. The \texttt{\&\&} operators
run each next step only after the preceding command succeeds.
If the final line does not show the new practice directory, stop;
do not run key generation from your existing directory.
This key pair is a disposable teaching prop, never to be enrolled.

\begin{terminal}
keylab=$(mktemp -d /tmp/utp-key.XXXXXXXX) &&
  cd "$keylab" &&
  pwd
\end{terminal}

\cmd|ssh-keygen -t| selects the key type (here Ed25519, a common choice);
\texttt{-C} sets a synthetic comment; \texttt{-f} picks a filename inside
the practice directory. \texttt{-N ''} assigns an empty passphrase
only to this fixture so the exercise isn't interactive. Don't use that
option for a reusable personal key. If a file already exists,
decline to overwrite it and get out of the directory before
investigating where you are.

\begin{terminal}
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C practice-only -f ./demo-key -N ''
\end{terminal}

You can list names without displaying the private file. The next command
prints a fingerprint from the public file: \texttt{-l} lists fingerprint
information and \texttt{-f} names the input. Expect the key size, a
digest string, the practice comment, and the key type. Since the private
data were generated afresh, there's no correct literal digest to match
with a classmate. A second fingerprint of the same public file should
be identical. Generating this pair does not change your \texttt{.ssh}
directory or make it possible to sign in to any service.

\begin{terminal}
ls -l ./demo-key ./demo-key.pub
ssh-keygen -lf ./demo-key.pub
\end{terminal}

For a sign-in to an authorized host, the provider might instruct you to
choose an identity file with SSH's lowercase \texttt{-i} option. That is
a path to the local private key that SSH should use, not an instruction
to transfer it. There is no need to practice with a personal private
file, adjust agent forwarding, or export keys to another machine to
learn this distinction.

Password, passphrase, and confirmation prompt are easy to confuse
when everything is printed in the same window. A prompt to unlock your
identity file usually refers to your local key; a prompt for the named
account's password normally refers to the remote account. The client
may attempt more than one authentication method; read each prompt
instead of trying the same secret until it works. If the prompt
doesn't agree with the instructions that came with the account,
stop and ask the provider which methods should be enabled. Repeated
attempts can trigger an account lock or make the relevant failures
harder to spot in a support log.

If a real private key may have been disclosed, telling the provider
quickly matters more than renaming it on the laptop. The provider can
help revoke the associated authorization and give you the approved
replacement steps. Deleting a local copy is not a command to all
servers to stop accepting the corresponding public key. For the
practice pair no authorization exists, which is why you can remove
both fixture files without coordinating a service-side change.

\section{Notice when the shell changes rooms}
On an authorized account, once signed in, use a harmless context check
before editing anything. \cmd|pwd| shows the working directory and
\cmd|id -un| asks \cmd|id| for the name (\texttt{-n}) associated with
the current effective user ID (\texttt{-u}). These commands would run
on the computer that is currently hosting the shell. The example output
is fictional, printed here with no prompt to copy.

\begin{terminal}[title={At the authorized remote prompt}]
pwd
id -un
\end{terminal}
\begin{terminaloutput}
/home/learner
learner
\end{terminaloutput}

Compare these with your local results. Both accounts could have the same
name or directory layout: even a perfect literal match does not prove
you are in the local shell. A useful additional check is the
\cmd|hostname| observation from Chapter~\ref{ch:37}, considered alongside
which connection you opened. Mark the local and remote terminal tabs
differently if the application supports it. Avoid opening a second
login inside the first one while you are learning: you can end up
leaving only the inner shell and mistake the outer remote shell for home.

Before running a command copied from instructions, check which shell
is meant to see its metacharacters. For instance, a redirection entered
at the local prompt belongs to that local shell, even if the program
whose output you're capturing is an SSH client. A redirection typed
after you've signed in at the remote prompt belongs to the remote shell
instead. The previous chapters on quoting and redirection still apply;
now the shell interpreting the notation may be on a different machine.
Figure~\ref{fig:38-shells} shows why the original shell is still
there to receive commands after SSH finishes.
For this first login, keep the phases separate. Type the login command
by itself, confirm the remote context, and then enter one harmless
inspection command at a time. Do not paste blocks that assume you got
past a password or fingerprint prompt. Unexpected results become
much easier to recover from if the next operation hasn't already begun.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering\input{figures/chapter-38/shells.tikz}
\caption{The same window can show two different shells at different times.}\label{fig:38-shells}
\VisualDescription{The local shell starts an SSH client, which connects to a remote shell. Exiting the remote shell returns control to the local one.}
\end{figure}

When done, \cmd|exit| at the remote shell normally ends that shell
and hands control back to your original local shell. Use \cmd|pwd|
again to confirm you are back. If SSH is executing one remote command
rather than starting a shell, its exit status ordinarily reflects
the remote command; a client error is reported as 255. The command may
itself choose 255, so the number alone doesn't diagnose the failure.
Read the actual error before drawing a conclusion.

Sometimes the support desk's first question is whether the account allows
an interactive shell at all. A file-transfer-only account need not
give you one; it may authenticate and reject an attempt to start an
interactive session. Ask whether a shell is included rather than trying
to get around the limitation. When reporting a failed login, note
whether you saw the expected host key and whether you reached the
provider's expected account authentication prompt. Share only the
relevant sanitized error and context, never your password, private key,
one-time code, or recovery credentials. The wording of a particular
error can change from one client version to another: match the failure
stage first.

\begin{terminal}[title={Run exit remotely; run pwd after the local prompt returns}]
exit
pwd
\end{terminal}

\begin{quicktable}{p{1.4in}Y}
\toprule
Symptom & What it does and doesn't establish \\
\midrule
Cannot resolve host & You have not located the destination by name; recheck the supplied address and connection prerequisites. \\
Host identification changed & A formerly recorded identity differs, not necessarily because of an attack; independently verify before continuing. \\
Permission denied on sign-in & The server did not accept an available account authentication method; don't loop over guessed passwords. \\
Remote prompt appears & A session started; still check the account and directories before manipulating files. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\paragraph{Key lab cleanup.}
After the exercises, if you created the fixture key pair, check that
\cmd|pwd| prints your key lab directory, not your real key directory.
\cmd|rm -i| prompts for each removal; confirm only the synthetic
files listed below. Use \cmd|rmdir| only after leaving the lab;
it refuses to remove a nonempty directory. This is routine
workspace cleanup, not secure erasure.

\begin{terminal}
pwd
rm -i ./demo-key ./demo-key.pub
cd && rmdir "$keylab"
\end{terminal}

\begin{exercise}{Read before dialing}\label{ex:38-offline}
Change the user and port in the offline \texttt{-G} example to
\texttt{student} and \texttt{2022}; leave \texttt{-F /dev/null},
\texttt{-G}, and \texttt{-T} in place. Check the fields; the exercise creates no files
and should not attempt a connection.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
ssh -F /dev/null -G -T -p 2022 student@unix.example.org |
  grep -E '^(host|user|port) '
\end{terminal}
Look for \texttt{user student} and \texttt{port 2022}; the host has not changed.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Fingerprint a fixture twice}\label{ex:38-fixture}
Run the fingerprint command from the practice directory twice.
Compare the fingerprint field and identify the disposable comment.
Leave both files in place until the final cleanup. If key generation
is unavailable on your client, explain the intended comparison instead.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\cmd|ssh-keygen -lf ./demo-key.pub| gives the same fingerprint both
times for the same public key. \texttt{practice-only} is the comment,
not the key or the fingerprint.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Choose what to disclose}\label{ex:38-disclose}
Write on paper which of the two practice filenames a provider would
normally request in a key enrollment, and which must not be sent.
Then remove the practice files and directory. Do not send either file.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
The public file is \texttt{demo-key.pub}; \texttt{demo-key} is private.
Follow the key lab cleanup steps above without sending either file.
\end{solution}

\begin{takeaways}
\item Get the destination, expected host keys, and account details from the provider.
\item A host key identifies the server; a user credential authenticates the account.
\item Check which shell is active before touching files; leave the remote shell explicitly.
\end{takeaways}

